1
00:00:00,371 --> 00:00:08,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Welcome everyone to the 34th Middlesex State Representative Democratic Debate hosted by the Somerville Democratic Party.

2
00:00:08,757 --> 00:00:11,379
[SPEAKER_01]: It is being filmed by the Somerville Media Center.

3
00:00:12,219 --> 00:00:17,683
[SPEAKER_01]: This district is made up of West Somerville, Winter Hill, and South Medford.

4
00:00:18,504 --> 00:00:27,209
[SPEAKER_01]: It is currently held by Christine Barber who is not seeking re-election in order to run for the second Middlesex Senate seat.

5
00:00:28,210 --> 00:00:41,482
[SPEAKER_01]: No Republican, third party, or unenrolled candidate qualified for the November general election, so the Democratic Party primary is almost certain to decide the next state rep.

6
00:00:42,303 --> 00:00:51,972
[SPEAKER_01]: All registered Democrats and unenrolled voters can vote in this election on or before September 1st by requesting a Democratic primary ballot.

7
00:00:53,240 --> 00:01:02,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Our candidates tonight for the 34th Middlesex District are Somerville City Councilor-at-Large, Will Embaugh.

8
00:01:04,845 --> 00:01:06,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure.

9
00:01:08,487 --> 00:01:11,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Somerville Ward 4 resident, Chris Oates.

10
00:01:16,471 --> 00:01:20,152
[SPEAKER_01]: And Medford School Committee person, Paul Rousseau.

11
00:01:20,893 --> 00:01:21,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Welcome gentlemen.

12
00:01:25,661 --> 00:01:32,183
[SPEAKER_01]: The rules for tonight's debate were crafted by the Democratic Party here in Somerville, and they are as follows.

13
00:01:32,963 --> 00:01:36,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Each candidate will get a three-minute opening statement.

14
00:01:36,865 --> 00:01:43,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Candidates will have one minute and 30 seconds for a direct response and 30 seconds for follow-ups.

15
00:01:44,968 --> 00:01:49,029
[SPEAKER_01]: We will begin in the order that they are standing to my left.

16
00:01:50,990 --> 00:01:56,575
[SPEAKER_01]: The podiums were arranged by random drawing, and we will begin in the order of which they are standing.

17
00:01:57,155 --> 00:02:02,719
[SPEAKER_01]: So we're going to begin with Councilor Mba's three-minute opening statement.

18
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:03,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Mba.

19
00:02:05,181 --> 00:02:05,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Joe.

20
00:02:06,723 --> 00:02:09,045
[SPEAKER_00]: I appreciate the opportunity.

21
00:02:09,105 --> 00:02:09,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

22
00:02:10,265 --> 00:02:13,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Jack and the Somerville Dems for your leadership in bringing us together.

23
00:02:14,108 --> 00:02:18,191
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm pretty sure my colleague, Chris, you will be excited about this.

24
00:02:18,271 --> 00:02:22,355
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been asking for this debate for a very long time, and Paula and I, we're super excited.

25
00:02:23,435 --> 00:02:28,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Before I also say a few words, just the residents of the district, thank you for being here.

26
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:30,901
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'm really excited.

27
00:02:30,941 --> 00:02:32,002
[SPEAKER_00]: You guys are our bosses.

28
00:02:32,182 --> 00:02:33,203
[SPEAKER_00]: My name is William Barr.

29
00:02:33,764 --> 00:02:37,687
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm running to be your next state representative for the 34th Middlesex District.

30
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:43,043
[SPEAKER_00]: 16 years ago, I came to this country from Cameroon in search of opportunity.

31
00:02:43,983 --> 00:02:51,927
[SPEAKER_00]: And like many families around the district, you know, I have, you know, my own, I have experienced my own share, you know, of the struggle.

32
00:02:51,987 --> 00:02:55,129
[SPEAKER_00]: I moved five times in six years in search of affordable housing.

33
00:02:55,869 --> 00:02:59,712
[SPEAKER_00]: And my wife and I, we are raising our kids in West Somerville that are in public schools.

34
00:03:00,332 --> 00:03:01,212
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, they love it.

35
00:03:01,653 --> 00:03:07,676
[SPEAKER_00]: And every day we also keep wondering for how long these kids will continue to enjoy this community that they love.

36
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:14,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So those experiences have shaped how I approach public service.

37
00:03:15,051 --> 00:03:23,336
[SPEAKER_00]: And for nearly a decade, you have been able to entrust me with the responsibility of leading this community as your counselor at large.

38
00:03:24,176 --> 00:03:29,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And with that, we've delivered a lot of meaningful results and not just talk about problems.

39
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,321
[SPEAKER_00]: I've helped create the Office of Housing Stability.

40
00:03:33,242 --> 00:03:34,882
[SPEAKER_00]: and the Somerville Community Land Trust.

41
00:03:35,442 --> 00:03:37,863
[SPEAKER_00]: I've led effort to protect workers from waste theft.

42
00:03:38,583 --> 00:03:51,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I've championed climate action, safe streets, environmental justice, immigrant rights, and investment in affordable housing through my position on the Job Creation and Retention Trust and also on the Affordable Housing Trust.

43
00:03:52,506 --> 00:03:55,706
[SPEAKER_00]: This race isn't simply about the best ideas.

44
00:03:56,446 --> 00:04:00,387
[SPEAKER_00]: It is about who is ready to turn those ideas into law on Beacon Hill.

45
00:04:01,566 --> 00:04:15,393
[SPEAKER_00]: There are absolutely no doubt, I have a tremendous respect for my colleagues, Chris Oates and Paul Russell, amazing gentlemen who I greatly respect, and for also just your willingness to serve.

46
00:04:16,113 --> 00:04:18,194
[SPEAKER_00]: But I have something different.

47
00:04:18,975 --> 00:04:27,479
[SPEAKER_00]: It is about a proven track record of governing, writing legislation, building coalitions, and passing policies, and delivering results on day one.

48
00:04:28,252 --> 00:04:30,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So I won't need to learn about how government works.

49
00:04:30,894 --> 00:04:36,058
[SPEAKER_00]: We need somebody who understands city hall, and then we can also take that on Beacon Hill.

50
00:04:36,559 --> 00:04:46,447
[SPEAKER_00]: So all that is to say at a state house, I will fight to make Massachusetts more affordable by building more affordable housing, strengthening tenant protections,

51
00:04:47,007 --> 00:04:53,649
[SPEAKER_00]: improving transportation, fully funding our public schools, protecting workers' rights, expanding access to health care.

52
00:04:53,950 --> 00:04:56,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And I have personal stories behind all these issues.

53
00:04:56,931 --> 00:05:02,172
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, advancing climate action and ensuring that every family has a fair opportunity to succeed.

54
00:05:02,813 --> 00:05:08,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is what I'm going to bring, and I respectfully, you know, ask for your vote on September 1st.

55
00:05:08,655 --> 00:05:11,976
[SPEAKER_00]: So thank you for being here, and I look forward to the conversation as we unfold.

56
00:05:12,436 --> 00:05:13,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Will and Bah.

57
00:05:13,516 --> 00:05:14,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Next up, Chris Oates.

58
00:05:15,172 --> 00:05:15,772
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, thank you.

59
00:05:15,892 --> 00:05:17,393
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you everyone for being here.

60
00:05:17,413 --> 00:05:18,194
[SPEAKER_03]: My name is Chris Oates.

61
00:05:18,274 --> 00:05:23,877
[SPEAKER_03]: It is lovely to share a little bit of democracy with you here tonight because we don't have enough of that in Massachusetts.

62
00:05:24,338 --> 00:05:28,500
[SPEAKER_03]: Massachusetts has the least competitive state legislature in the entire country.

63
00:05:29,141 --> 00:05:32,163
[SPEAKER_03]: Most of our state legislative seats this year are uncontested.

64
00:05:32,583 --> 00:05:36,946
[SPEAKER_03]: Many of the others will be decided on September 1st before a lot of people start tuning in like this one.

65
00:05:37,566 --> 00:05:39,988
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is not how democracy should work.

66
00:05:40,048 --> 00:05:43,490
[SPEAKER_03]: And a lot of the issues that we're gonna be talking about tonight from transportation to childcare,

67
00:05:43,870 --> 00:05:44,371
[SPEAKER_03]: to energy.

68
00:05:44,491 --> 00:05:50,856
[SPEAKER_03]: One of the reasons we don't see the urgency on Beacon Hill is because we don't have that popular pressure on our elected officials.

69
00:05:51,296 --> 00:05:54,139
[SPEAKER_03]: Now I never really expected to be up here running for state representative.

70
00:05:54,239 --> 00:05:56,020
[SPEAKER_03]: I thought my career was going to be in foreign policy.

71
00:05:56,521 --> 00:06:03,626
[SPEAKER_03]: I was 17 when the Iraq war started and that led me to study political science at Brown and then go to Oxford for my master's and PhD in international relations.

72
00:06:04,307 --> 00:06:06,529
[SPEAKER_03]: After that I got a job as a political risk analyst so I was writing

73
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:10,282
[SPEAKER_03]: The mic's not working, sorry.

74
00:06:11,243 --> 00:06:21,772
[SPEAKER_03]: I worked as a political risk analyst, which was writing reports, pretty lengthy ones, advising governments and multinational organizations about everything from geopolitics to macroeconomics.

75
00:06:21,913 --> 00:06:28,438
[SPEAKER_03]: But I started to notice that a lot of the stuff in my work, and then the things that I was living personally, were not being decided in Washington.

76
00:06:28,478 --> 00:06:30,200
[SPEAKER_03]: They were being decided at the state level.

77
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:38,684
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is the housing that I was trying to afford, the traffic that my dad was sitting in every day on his way to work, or the mental health care that my sister has unfortunately had to have a lot of.

78
00:06:39,485 --> 00:06:45,508
[SPEAKER_03]: And yet there was still less scrutiny of our state governments often than when a presidential candidate had breakfast in New Hampshire.

79
00:06:46,008 --> 00:06:47,209
[SPEAKER_03]: And so I thought that needed to change.

80
00:06:47,249 --> 00:06:51,852
[SPEAKER_03]: So I joined the Ranked Choice Voting campaign in 2018 to try to bring more choice to our elections.

81
00:06:52,293 --> 00:06:57,777
[SPEAKER_03]: I helped lead the statewide coalition, which is really the only way you get anything done on Beacon Hill, is to have a statewide coalition.

82
00:06:58,117 --> 00:07:02,620
[SPEAKER_03]: And when that campaign ended, I started a company to make state legislatures across the country more accessible.

83
00:07:03,001 --> 00:07:06,023
[SPEAKER_03]: But for me, the big change in my life was when I had a son.

84
00:07:06,203 --> 00:07:08,224
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, if my wife had a son, I was there.

85
00:07:08,925 --> 00:07:10,286
[SPEAKER_03]: I ate a lot of gummy bears that day.

86
00:07:11,447 --> 00:07:15,130
[SPEAKER_03]: But then when she went back to work, my wife's a doctor, so I became the primary caregiver.

87
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:26,075
[SPEAKER_03]: And what I saw was that all of the mailing lists that I had signed up to about come to this event, go to that event, they were all happening during bedtime and bath time and story time.

88
00:07:26,536 --> 00:07:27,336
[SPEAKER_03]: And I couldn't go to them.

89
00:07:27,496 --> 00:07:38,042
[SPEAKER_03]: And you see that when our elections are uncompetitive and when so many of the civic events that have the conversations that shape the decisions that affect government are happening at a time where many working families can't attend,

90
00:07:38,602 --> 00:07:39,543
[SPEAKER_03]: you really are shut out.

91
00:07:39,623 --> 00:07:55,643
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's really why I'm running today, because I think what we need from our next representative is someone who can understand the complexities of the policies, of the full spectrum policies, can build the statewide coalitions, because you can't get anything done on Beacon Hill unless you have a geographic and ideological breadth in your coalition.

92
00:07:56,083 --> 00:08:02,965
[SPEAKER_03]: and can always remember that the people in the room with them, the people donating, the people coming to events like this, are not the totality of our district.

93
00:08:03,005 --> 00:08:12,588
[SPEAKER_03]: And so our job is to always be reaching out to communities that we feel are not already involved in the political process, finding out what they need, and then delivering the results to serve them.

94
00:08:12,948 --> 00:08:13,208
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

95
00:08:14,128 --> 00:08:15,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Chris Oates.

96
00:08:15,309 --> 00:08:16,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Up next, Paul Rousseau.

97
00:08:17,968 --> 00:08:19,129
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you, everyone, for being here.

98
00:08:19,189 --> 00:08:20,451
[SPEAKER_02]: I hope I can project enough.

99
00:08:21,391 --> 00:08:29,139
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm sure this is how everybody wanted to spend their Saturday, but I do really appreciate the Somerville Democratic City Committee putting this together.

100
00:08:29,159 --> 00:08:31,001
[SPEAKER_02]: My name's Paul Rousseau.

101
00:08:31,081 --> 00:08:33,724
[SPEAKER_02]: I am running to be your next state representative, well, some of you.

102
00:08:34,965 --> 00:08:39,330
[SPEAKER_02]: And some of you know me, some of you don't, and I'm really glad that you're here regardless.

103
00:08:40,297 --> 00:08:42,920
[SPEAKER_02]: I've lived in the Medford-Somerville area for over 20 years.

104
00:08:44,121 --> 00:08:51,869
[SPEAKER_02]: I first lived in Somerville on Powder House Boulevard, but I now live in Medford with my husband, Bob, and our two kids, Nev and Matthew.

105
00:08:51,929 --> 00:08:54,252
[SPEAKER_02]: They're now adults, so I don't know what to call them.

106
00:08:55,042 --> 00:08:57,825
[SPEAKER_02]: and of course, our cats.

107
00:08:58,025 --> 00:09:06,574
[SPEAKER_02]: By day, I'm a software engineer at Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, supporting technology that is behind life-saving research, cancer research.

108
00:09:07,375 --> 00:09:14,382
[SPEAKER_02]: For the last nine years, I have had the honor to serve on the Medford School Committee, where I am proud to have transformed our schools in that time.

109
00:09:16,848 --> 00:09:31,692
[SPEAKER_02]: This last term, I co-led the 2024-2025 Medford Teacher Contract negotiations, which got us smaller class sizes, paid parental leave, and the largest raise in Medford Teachers Association's history.

110
00:09:32,592 --> 00:09:37,073
[SPEAKER_02]: We more than doubled the pay for our paraprofessionals, and that was at my demand.

111
00:09:37,273 --> 00:09:41,534
[SPEAKER_02]: When I first was elected and I saw what our paraprofessionals made, I was beyond

112
00:09:42,420 --> 00:09:46,726
[SPEAKER_02]: mortified, and now this year they get another 8%, next year another 8%.

113
00:09:47,487 --> 00:09:49,070
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not a living wage, but we're getting there.

114
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:53,903
[SPEAKER_02]: I brought free meals to our district before the state did.

115
00:09:53,923 --> 00:09:57,706
[SPEAKER_02]: I brought free dental checkups in our elementary schools.

116
00:09:57,766 --> 00:10:00,808
[SPEAKER_02]: A lot of these things have a very direct connection to my own experience.

117
00:10:01,648 --> 00:10:06,312
[SPEAKER_02]: I know how to get things done in government, and I also know firsthand what it feels like when government fails.

118
00:10:07,132 --> 00:10:12,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Far too many people in Medford, Somerville, and across Massachusetts also know what it's like when the government fails.

119
00:10:14,017 --> 00:10:18,901
[SPEAKER_02]: I grew up with homelessness, food insecurity, and all the other things that go with that.

120
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:23,662
[SPEAKER_02]: These are not just failures of government.

121
00:10:23,702 --> 00:10:25,843
[SPEAKER_02]: They are purposeful policy decisions.

122
00:10:26,144 --> 00:10:31,807
[SPEAKER_02]: The way we craft our policy to make, frankly, a lot of liberals feel good that we're doing the right thing.

123
00:10:32,167 --> 00:10:36,530
[SPEAKER_02]: And that stuff doesn't actually land where the people that need it get it.

124
00:10:37,732 --> 00:10:42,355
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that is the kind of business as usual that I'm not looking forward to continuing at the Statehouse.

125
00:10:44,036 --> 00:10:49,920
[SPEAKER_02]: One of my favorite things is that Massachusetts is really the best at sustaining the unsustainable.

126
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:57,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Every year we hear about our healthcare costs being unsustainable, and it's been going on now for most of everybody here's lives.

127
00:10:58,985 --> 00:10:59,986
[SPEAKER_02]: Sounds pretty sustainable.

128
00:11:01,252 --> 00:11:04,175
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's, of course, why Medicare for All is my number one priority.

129
00:11:04,976 --> 00:11:07,919
[SPEAKER_02]: And I'm going to run out of time before my list is done.

130
00:11:07,979 --> 00:11:13,285
[SPEAKER_02]: But I really appreciate everybody being here and listening, and I look forward to answering a lot more questions tonight.

131
00:11:13,705 --> 00:11:14,966
[SPEAKER_02]: And thank you so much.

132
00:11:15,767 --> 00:11:16,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Paul Russo.

133
00:11:16,908 --> 00:11:19,010
[SPEAKER_01]: We're going on to the Q&A portion.

134
00:11:19,431 --> 00:11:20,151
[SPEAKER_01]: First question.

135
00:11:21,062 --> 00:11:29,433
[SPEAKER_01]: The three of you, all progressive Democrats, are seeking the same office and you agree on many of the problems facing this district.

136
00:11:30,114 --> 00:11:38,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Many voters are looking not just who agrees with them, but who is most likely to successfully legislate on them.

137
00:11:38,945 --> 00:11:49,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you each speak to what kind of legislative style you will take and how you intend to be the most effective legislator for this district?

138
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:50,550
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm going to start.

139
00:11:50,670 --> 00:11:53,171
[SPEAKER_01]: These are one-minute, 15-second answers.

140
00:11:53,672 --> 00:11:57,713
[SPEAKER_01]: They are very good about keeping time because the timer is right in front of them.

141
00:11:59,854 --> 00:12:06,217
[SPEAKER_01]: So we're going to start with Councillor Imba going to Mr. Oates and then Mr. Russo, and we'll start changing up after that.

142
00:12:06,877 --> 00:12:07,818
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Councillor Imba.

143
00:12:08,913 --> 00:12:10,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Joe, for that question.

144
00:12:11,718 --> 00:12:20,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Just like I mentioned in my introduction that I have tremendous respect for my, you know, counterpart, and I actually, you know, started differentiating myself.

145
00:12:21,463 --> 00:12:29,546
[SPEAKER_00]: about the fact that I have a proven track record of somebody who has been able to translate ideas into policy and actually govern.

146
00:12:29,566 --> 00:12:30,706
[SPEAKER_00]: So I understand City Hall.

147
00:12:31,127 --> 00:12:38,009
[SPEAKER_00]: I know most of the legislation that left from Somerville and they are waiting at Beacon Hill, you know, for us to kind of like push them forward.

148
00:12:38,489 --> 00:12:41,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So I have my own lived experiences on top of that.

149
00:12:41,731 --> 00:12:48,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is like I bring, you know, a very holistic approach because it's like they're saying that whoever is closest

150
00:12:48,848 --> 00:12:51,089
[SPEAKER_00]: you know, to the pen should be closest to the power.

151
00:12:51,209 --> 00:12:54,732
[SPEAKER_00]: So I have, you know, experienced most of these issues personally.

152
00:12:54,752 --> 00:12:58,534
[SPEAKER_00]: I have spoken on most of the legislation at the Statehouse on housing.

153
00:12:59,034 --> 00:13:01,756
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a frequent flyer at the Statehouse, spoken on housing.

154
00:13:02,123 --> 00:13:12,668
[SPEAKER_00]: co-founded Somerville Alliance for Safe Street, Champion for Safe Street, spoken on climate legislation on Beacon Hill, spoken on immigrant legislation, the Safe Community Act, we just passed the PROTECT Act.

155
00:13:13,068 --> 00:13:16,890
[SPEAKER_00]: I have been on Beacon Hill on all this legislation.

156
00:13:16,950 --> 00:13:19,551
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that is something that differentiates me.

157
00:13:19,591 --> 00:13:24,373
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't just show up, you know, I show up, I get the job done, I build coalition,

158
00:13:24,917 --> 00:13:28,200
[SPEAKER_00]: I know how to weave the system because this is how it takes.

159
00:13:28,541 --> 00:13:40,812
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a community guy who loves this community, loves Medford, and I have a contingent of Medford constituents right here to see how we will be having this conversation in broad daylight and how we can differentiate ourselves.

160
00:13:40,852 --> 00:13:42,434
[SPEAKER_00]: So I appreciate the opportunity.

161
00:13:42,734 --> 00:13:43,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Will.

162
00:13:43,335 --> 00:13:43,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Chris?

163
00:13:44,216 --> 00:13:51,145
[SPEAKER_03]: So I worked on the rank choice voting campaign and my title was senior advisor for strategic partnerships, which was the most pretentious title I've ever had in my life.

164
00:13:51,826 --> 00:13:57,513
[SPEAKER_03]: And it was basically, go out and build the coalition that can get rank choice voting passed.

165
00:13:57,894 --> 00:13:59,916
[SPEAKER_03]: We had four pieces of legislation on Beacon Hill.

166
00:14:00,717 --> 00:14:01,977
[SPEAKER_03]: And we also had a ballot initiative.

167
00:14:01,997 --> 00:14:03,338
[SPEAKER_03]: And we're trying to do both at the same time.

168
00:14:03,398 --> 00:14:06,238
[SPEAKER_03]: Obviously, if you can get the legislation passed, you don't have to go to the ballot.

169
00:14:06,739 --> 00:14:09,179
[SPEAKER_03]: And what I saw was, it's very simple.

170
00:14:09,219 --> 00:14:10,780
[SPEAKER_03]: You need in the House 81 votes.

171
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:11,740
[SPEAKER_03]: You need in the Senate 21.

172
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:17,041
[SPEAKER_03]: So you cannot just say, this is popular in Somerville or Medford or Cambridge or Boston.

173
00:14:17,361 --> 00:14:19,102
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to get allies.

174
00:14:20,022 --> 00:14:22,443
[SPEAKER_03]: in the Berkshires, in Worcester County, in the South Coast.

175
00:14:22,683 --> 00:14:27,525
[SPEAKER_03]: And what we had to do was basically go to a very wide range of people who did not agree on everything.

176
00:14:27,745 --> 00:14:28,645
[SPEAKER_03]: They didn't agree on much.

177
00:14:29,105 --> 00:14:33,387
[SPEAKER_03]: But if we could get them to agree on this one issue, then we could move it forward.

178
00:14:33,707 --> 00:14:40,609
[SPEAKER_03]: And what I noticed that the best way to do that is obviously you go in there and you try to persuade them on the facts and you try to find out where their interests align with your interests.

179
00:14:40,909 --> 00:14:45,411
[SPEAKER_03]: But the single best way is to do it with a coalition of advocacy groups across the state.

180
00:14:45,711 --> 00:14:50,213
[SPEAKER_03]: Because when we try to go in and meet with a representative, it was off me go, sorry, we're really busy.

181
00:14:50,533 --> 00:14:55,675
[SPEAKER_03]: When we said, you have five of your constituents who want to go in and meet with you, all of a sudden they had that meeting booked.

182
00:14:55,975 --> 00:14:57,516
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's really how I see it.

183
00:14:57,616 --> 00:15:02,978
[SPEAKER_03]: Work with the advocacy groups across the state and say, what do you need from me inside the building?

184
00:15:03,618 --> 00:15:18,009
[SPEAKER_03]: to help you on the outside, whether that is some strategic guidance about where the, you know, the critical levers are, or whether it's just to be that kind of good cop in the building so after they meet with their constituents, they can come to me and say, what's really going on, and they can hopefully trust me.

185
00:15:18,809 --> 00:15:19,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Chris.

186
00:15:19,490 --> 00:15:19,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Paul?

187
00:15:20,850 --> 00:15:22,171
[SPEAKER_02]: So I love this question a lot.

188
00:15:23,212 --> 00:15:25,833
[SPEAKER_02]: So I was the first Our Revolution Medford candidate in 2017.

189
00:15:26,694 --> 00:15:29,035
[SPEAKER_02]: I was sworn in in 2018.

190
00:15:29,696 --> 00:15:36,300
[SPEAKER_02]: And in that time, the coalition has grown so that all of the school committee members are on that slate.

191
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:39,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Six of the seven city councilors are on that slate.

192
00:15:39,682 --> 00:15:50,106
[SPEAKER_02]: I know how to build a coalition, keep it together, which is an unrelated activity, frankly, and ensure that we are all staying true to the thing that brings us together.

193
00:15:50,306 --> 00:15:55,508
[SPEAKER_02]: And what made our revolution in Medford so successful was that we didn't have a big pile of policies.

194
00:15:55,828 --> 00:15:57,229
[SPEAKER_02]: We had value statements.

195
00:15:57,689 --> 00:16:03,992
[SPEAKER_02]: So somebody else was talking about this earlier, but we've got to talk about our values because policy is

196
00:16:04,872 --> 00:16:10,814
[SPEAKER_02]: frankly, very detailed and is hard to digest for most people, but values are not.

197
00:16:11,214 --> 00:16:18,577
[SPEAKER_02]: This coalition has 12 of the 14 local officials all on the same slate every single election.

198
00:16:19,257 --> 00:16:22,038
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is not a testament to a click.

199
00:16:22,438 --> 00:16:26,600
[SPEAKER_02]: That is a testament to the fact that we said we would do something, and we've done it.

200
00:16:27,090 --> 00:16:28,170
[SPEAKER_02]: and we continue to do it.

201
00:16:28,250 --> 00:16:35,472
[SPEAKER_02]: And when I say Medford Public Schools has changed dramatically in nine years, I don't think you can comprehend how dramatically it has changed.

202
00:16:35,932 --> 00:16:40,054
[SPEAKER_02]: It is an unrecognizable school system after five terms in office.

203
00:16:40,634 --> 00:16:52,537
[SPEAKER_02]: And I plan to take that same approach to the State House, where I'm going to subvert my other 750 priorities to the ones that are most important now, like Medicare for All.

204
00:16:54,377 --> 00:16:54,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Paul.

205
00:16:55,117 --> 00:16:58,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So in one word, gentlemen, this is the moderator's discretion.

206
00:16:59,359 --> 00:16:59,879
[SPEAKER_01]: One word.

207
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:02,500
[SPEAKER_01]: What is your legislative style?

208
00:17:02,921 --> 00:17:03,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go to Paul first.

209
00:17:07,142 --> 00:17:07,723
[SPEAKER_01]: One word.

210
00:17:08,503 --> 00:17:11,444
[SPEAKER_02]: Prepared for five minute long answers.

211
00:17:12,385 --> 00:17:14,445
[SPEAKER_02]: I would say coalitions.

212
00:17:15,286 --> 00:17:15,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Chris.

213
00:17:16,186 --> 00:17:17,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, he stole mine.

214
00:17:17,047 --> 00:17:18,087
[SPEAKER_01]: I was going to say coalitional.

215
00:17:18,107 --> 00:17:18,727
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

216
00:17:19,628 --> 00:17:21,068
[SPEAKER_01]: You can both have the same answer.

217
00:17:21,108 --> 00:17:21,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Will.

218
00:17:21,829 --> 00:17:22,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Chris stole mine.

219
00:17:23,267 --> 00:17:25,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Corporation, corporation, I would just say.

220
00:17:25,389 --> 00:17:26,750
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

221
00:17:27,350 --> 00:17:30,533
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm looking for some debate here, folks, but let's go to question two.

222
00:17:30,593 --> 00:17:31,954
[SPEAKER_00]: I say corporation for myself.

223
00:17:32,435 --> 00:17:41,642
[SPEAKER_01]: On which one legislative committee would you most like to serve, why, and how do you expect to get that assignment?

224
00:17:42,162 --> 00:17:43,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go to Mr. Rousseau first.

225
00:17:44,264 --> 00:17:52,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, so I'll be quick with the answer that is my real answer, and I know that I can't have it, so I'm going to move on past that.

226
00:17:52,274 --> 00:17:54,237
[SPEAKER_02]: The first one I would really want is rules.

227
00:17:55,058 --> 00:18:02,567
[SPEAKER_02]: I wrote all the rules for Medford's public schools, the school committee, there were none when I was elected, and I understand that rules are

228
00:18:03,248 --> 00:18:04,629
[SPEAKER_02]: not just a little thing.

229
00:18:04,769 --> 00:18:07,070
[SPEAKER_02]: They're really how the machinery works.

230
00:18:07,731 --> 00:18:10,132
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no way a freshman's getting on that committee.

231
00:18:11,092 --> 00:18:14,014
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's probably a 0% chance they don't stick me on education.

232
00:18:14,114 --> 00:18:15,135
[SPEAKER_02]: I care about education.

233
00:18:15,175 --> 00:18:18,917
[SPEAKER_02]: I've been a school committee member, so of course I'd be happy to be on education.

234
00:18:19,317 --> 00:18:22,659
[SPEAKER_02]: The one I really want to be on right now, though, is health care financing.

235
00:18:23,780 --> 00:18:27,001
[SPEAKER_02]: The title of it alone tells you that it is not about Medicare for All.

236
00:18:27,402 --> 00:18:28,002
[SPEAKER_02]: It is about

237
00:18:28,468 --> 00:18:33,594
[SPEAKER_02]: How do we deal with the freak out every year of the 10, 12, whatever percent increase that we have?

238
00:18:33,914 --> 00:18:38,059
[SPEAKER_02]: And every year we say we can't sustain this, and somehow we just keep sustaining it.

239
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:43,986
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I want to be on the Health Care Financing Committee because I would like to try to steer the conversation away from

240
00:18:44,917 --> 00:18:50,483
[SPEAKER_02]: not just the annual freakout, which we have to have because we have to figure it out, but it can't be the whole conversation.

241
00:18:50,643 --> 00:18:57,750
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's the whole conversation throughout the entire year, year after year, when do we talk about implementing Medicare for all?

242
00:18:57,850 --> 00:19:00,293
[SPEAKER_02]: So healthcare financing is my final answer.

243
00:19:01,493 --> 00:19:02,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

244
00:19:02,214 --> 00:19:03,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Counselor Mbah, how about you?

245
00:19:03,955 --> 00:19:04,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, thank you, Joe.

246
00:19:05,137 --> 00:19:16,128
[SPEAKER_00]: And just to mirror what Paul said, even myself, there's so many things I want to be on, besides housing, which everybody cares about housing.

247
00:19:16,608 --> 00:19:31,004
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been thinking a lot about, like, all this, like, recently the MBTA actually wasn't really working, so I was, when I was taking the bus, you know, by, I was like, oh, this is like, we can, I would love to be on this, on this committee.

248
00:19:31,024 --> 00:19:34,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I thought about my own healthcare, you know, experience in the United States.

249
00:19:34,948 --> 00:19:36,830
[SPEAKER_00]: When I moved here, I applied for MassHealth.

250
00:19:37,150 --> 00:19:37,971
[SPEAKER_00]: They rejected me.

251
00:19:38,011 --> 00:19:40,772
[SPEAKER_00]: They said I wasn't below 21, and I didn't have independent.

252
00:19:41,092 --> 00:19:43,934
[SPEAKER_00]: And then at the end of the year, they penalized me for not having health insurance.

253
00:19:43,974 --> 00:19:48,717
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm like, oh, I want to be on health care policies.

254
00:19:48,817 --> 00:19:52,699
[SPEAKER_00]: And so as an environmental scientist myself, also, they just passed the environmental bond bill.

255
00:19:53,000 --> 00:20:00,944
[SPEAKER_00]: So you need people to push for those funding into communities to enable them to do climate resilience and all these different things.

256
00:20:00,964 --> 00:20:03,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So there's all these different things I'm excited to be on.

257
00:20:03,966 --> 00:20:12,589
[SPEAKER_00]: immigration, I don't want to be the whistleblower, but again we just passed the PROTECT Act, but again there's a lot that we can do to actually push, you know, communities forward.

258
00:20:12,689 --> 00:20:19,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Mba, I'm not putting you on a committee so you don't have to look at me, but which one committee?

259
00:20:19,372 --> 00:20:23,953
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I want to be on, not one, I want to be on a few.

260
00:20:23,993 --> 00:20:27,474
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to be on housing and I want to be on environment.

261
00:20:28,295 --> 00:20:28,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

262
00:20:30,154 --> 00:20:30,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Who's up next?

263
00:20:30,734 --> 00:20:32,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, let's go to Chris Oates.

264
00:20:33,055 --> 00:20:38,497
[SPEAKER_03]: So I thought about this a lot because I feel like econ development fits with my background.

265
00:20:38,537 --> 00:20:41,317
[SPEAKER_03]: Housing is obviously something important, so is transportation for this district.

266
00:20:41,778 --> 00:20:46,759
[SPEAKER_03]: But as a first term representative, you don't get to choose the big committees like Ways and Means.

267
00:20:47,139 --> 00:20:51,461
[SPEAKER_03]: And so I've been thinking a lot about how do I make the biggest impact as a first term representative?

268
00:20:51,541 --> 00:20:58,603
[SPEAKER_03]: And what I think I want to do is be the person to go in there and say, what are the specific issues that have too often fallen through the cracks?

269
00:20:59,003 --> 00:21:05,767
[SPEAKER_03]: As everyone focuses on the big deal, the big ticket items, and a lot of folks on the committee chairs are pretty inundated.

270
00:21:05,807 --> 00:21:10,129
[SPEAKER_03]: So for me, that would be the Joint Committee on Children, Families, and Persons with Disabilities.

271
00:21:10,409 --> 00:21:13,391
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I feel like you have a really big chance to make an impact.

272
00:21:13,591 --> 00:21:25,257
[SPEAKER_03]: Even a small change can have huge implications for a family, or a child, or someone who is trying to get a handicap spot on their street.

273
00:21:25,577 --> 00:21:28,559
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the thing that is why you do this job.

274
00:21:29,290 --> 00:21:31,110
[SPEAKER_03]: So yeah, I still got 35 seconds.

275
00:21:31,451 --> 00:21:32,951
[SPEAKER_03]: Can I talk about econ development a little bit more?

276
00:21:32,971 --> 00:21:33,551
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure, you can.

277
00:21:33,731 --> 00:21:34,691
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your time.

278
00:21:34,711 --> 00:21:43,674
[SPEAKER_03]: So the other one is, if I can't get children's, it's econ dev because so much of probably what we're going to be talking about tonight is, oh, we're going to do this and we're going to pay for it.

279
00:21:44,514 --> 00:21:45,334
[SPEAKER_03]: How are we going to pay for it?

280
00:21:45,874 --> 00:21:50,956
[SPEAKER_03]: We're going to pay for it either by raising taxes, which is always a fight, or we could just pay for it through economic growth.

281
00:21:51,416 --> 00:21:53,857
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is a lot easier because you don't have to go into huge battles.

282
00:21:53,897 --> 00:21:55,357
[SPEAKER_03]: So that would probably be the other one.

283
00:21:55,777 --> 00:22:05,483
[SPEAKER_03]: If I can't get children, families, and persons with disabilities, is go for EconDev and try to build the economy that can pay for all the probably other things that we're going to be talking about tonight.

284
00:22:06,084 --> 00:22:07,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much, Chris.

285
00:22:07,244 --> 00:22:07,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Next question.

286
00:22:09,126 --> 00:22:26,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Imba, during your time on Somerville City Council, you voted to send Beacon Hill many home rule petitions, including on rent control, a real estate transfer fee, tenant opportunity to purchase, municipal voting rights for 16 and 17-year-olds, among others.

287
00:22:29,503 --> 00:22:32,764
[SPEAKER_01]: All of these proposals have languished in the legislature.

288
00:22:33,484 --> 00:22:43,188
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are elected, what specific tactics would you use to get Somerville and Medford's home rule petitions through the State House?

289
00:22:44,688 --> 00:22:45,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Joe.

290
00:22:45,688 --> 00:22:49,470
[SPEAKER_00]: I have been thinking about this and really been excited about it.

291
00:22:49,650 --> 00:22:51,830
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I even texted a few colleagues.

292
00:22:52,231 --> 00:22:56,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that we actually know the outcome, but there's been, you know,

293
00:22:57,188 --> 00:23:00,211
[SPEAKER_00]: excitement about how to push some of this legislation forward.

294
00:23:00,251 --> 00:23:05,515
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I talked about somebody who understands this, City Hall, and now is moving to Beacon Hill.

295
00:23:05,595 --> 00:23:08,238
[SPEAKER_00]: I think one of the things we need to do is education.

296
00:23:08,438 --> 00:23:17,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Our education and outreach methodology has to change, because you've got to bring people together so you understand that Medford and Somerville, these are two different communities.

297
00:23:17,506 --> 00:23:21,089
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been convincing in Medford, I've knocked thousands of doors there,

298
00:23:22,450 --> 00:23:33,221
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea about rent control is not very popular in Medford, and so that's why we have enabling legislation, you know, to give communities the option to do what it shows that works for their community.

299
00:23:33,241 --> 00:23:41,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is how you kind of like push legislation forward and bring meaningful change that is tailored to each community here in Massachusetts.

300
00:23:42,923 --> 00:23:43,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

301
00:23:43,924 --> 00:23:51,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Chris, if there's if there's a difference there between your style and Councilor and Boz, kind of highlight that.

302
00:23:51,172 --> 00:23:51,392
[SPEAKER_03]: Sure.

303
00:23:51,432 --> 00:23:59,662
[SPEAKER_03]: So I think the big reason why so many of our home rule petitions don't make it very far in the statehouse is because the state leaders think it would be bad for Massachusetts.

304
00:24:00,202 --> 00:24:02,804
[SPEAKER_03]: And really, you can say, well, it's just about Somerville.

305
00:24:02,824 --> 00:24:07,948
[SPEAKER_03]: But they say, well, if that has a negative impact on the Commonwealth overall, they're not going to support it.

306
00:24:08,328 --> 00:24:17,956
[SPEAKER_03]: So I think really the only way you do that is by building a reputation within the statehouse and with leadership that when you go to them and say, this is a home rule petition, but trust me, I've looked into it.

307
00:24:18,056 --> 00:24:19,477
[SPEAKER_03]: It is good for the state overall.

308
00:24:19,877 --> 00:24:21,838
[SPEAKER_03]: It is not something where we're going to do it.

309
00:24:22,219 --> 00:24:23,159
[SPEAKER_03]: It's going to be a disaster.

310
00:24:23,199 --> 00:24:24,681
[SPEAKER_03]: The state has to come in with extra money.

311
00:24:25,061 --> 00:24:27,323
[SPEAKER_03]: So you have to, I think, build that reputation of,

312
00:24:28,063 --> 00:24:33,445
[SPEAKER_03]: a serious thinker that when you go to them, it's not like you go to them every day and say, I need this, I need this, I need this.

313
00:24:33,785 --> 00:24:40,088
[SPEAKER_03]: You know when to pick and choose what you think is the most important for your district that you can then get through the leadership.

314
00:24:40,308 --> 00:24:45,150
[SPEAKER_03]: And that involves working a lot with the city officials and with advocacy groups to see that of the, you know,

315
00:24:46,350 --> 00:24:49,512
[SPEAKER_03]: 5,000 bills that get filed up there many of which are home rule petitions.

316
00:24:49,953 --> 00:25:08,885
[SPEAKER_03]: These are the ones that I'm really going to fight for because you have limited political capital up there and so you have to be able to understand and that is mainly through listening to others what it is that we really need to fight for and then you need to translate the demands of your community into why it is good for the state overall so that leadership on Beacon Hill will say yeah we'll go with this because we think that

317
00:25:09,325 --> 00:25:14,049
[SPEAKER_03]: even though it's a home rule petition, it will be good for the neighboring towns and it will be good for the overall state budget.

318
00:25:14,109 --> 00:25:15,610
[SPEAKER_03]: So I think that's the most important thing.

319
00:25:15,630 --> 00:25:22,315
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to demonstrate to leadership why this is not just for Somerville or for Medford, but really for Massachusetts overall.

320
00:25:23,090 --> 00:25:23,670
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you, Chris.

321
00:25:23,830 --> 00:25:26,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Paul, same style, different style?

322
00:25:26,791 --> 00:25:28,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Very different, very different style.

323
00:25:28,831 --> 00:25:29,632
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

324
00:25:30,052 --> 00:25:33,072
[SPEAKER_02]: I see the use of a lot of home rule petitions.

325
00:25:33,412 --> 00:25:39,074
[SPEAKER_02]: There are 511 of them filed so far this term as a failure of the legislature.

326
00:25:39,434 --> 00:25:46,195
[SPEAKER_02]: I had a great conversation on the door with a person who was very uncomfortable with the Somerville ballot question around divestment.

327
00:25:46,896 --> 00:25:52,397
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't agree with her, but I did agree that there's a problem with this use of

328
00:25:54,057 --> 00:26:00,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether it's ballot questions, I don't think that these local home rule petitions are terribly different, frankly.

329
00:26:00,541 --> 00:26:03,702
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a law, and then we want an exception.

330
00:26:04,503 --> 00:26:06,163
[SPEAKER_02]: That means the law's the problem.

331
00:26:06,824 --> 00:26:16,048
[SPEAKER_02]: And it is not, in my opinion, an approach where 27% of those local home rule petitions have been signed into law this term.

332
00:26:16,829 --> 00:26:21,571
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's a lot, considering only 43 real bills have actually been signed into law.

333
00:26:22,765 --> 00:26:24,125
[SPEAKER_02]: What is going on at the statehouse?

334
00:26:24,225 --> 00:26:26,046
[SPEAKER_02]: What's not going on is legislating.

335
00:26:26,946 --> 00:26:32,247
[SPEAKER_02]: With 5,000 bills filed in a single term, that is a tsunami of bills.

336
00:26:32,287 --> 00:26:39,328
[SPEAKER_02]: And so when I talk about wanting to get on the rules, one of the rules I want to do is to dramatically restrict the number of bills legislators can file.

337
00:26:39,848 --> 00:26:45,169
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is a mountain of stuff that has to be processed every term, it's not a mountain.

338
00:26:45,349 --> 00:26:47,269
[SPEAKER_02]: It's, you know, much bigger than a mountain.

339
00:26:47,969 --> 00:26:51,550
[SPEAKER_02]: How are we going to have the bandwidth to focus on things like Medicare for all?

340
00:26:52,867 --> 00:26:58,911
[SPEAKER_02]: We don't have the bandwidth, and I have a little sympathy, frankly, for the folks trying to keep the ship afloat.

341
00:27:00,552 --> 00:27:01,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

342
00:27:01,912 --> 00:27:02,853
[SPEAKER_01]: On to the next question.

343
00:27:04,765 --> 00:27:09,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Somerville and Medford have some of the highest costs of living expenses in the nation.

344
00:27:10,828 --> 00:27:16,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Lombard, you support allowing municipalities to adopt rent stabilization.

345
00:27:17,110 --> 00:27:24,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Oates, you have emphasized removing zoning and permitting barriers to private housing construction.

346
00:27:24,814 --> 00:27:31,877
[SPEAKER_01]: And Mr. Rousseau, you've called for a substantial expansion of publicly owned mixed income social housing.

347
00:27:32,919 --> 00:27:41,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Which of those approaches would reduce housing costs most quickly, and what evidence can you provide that will support your answer?

348
00:27:42,076 --> 00:27:43,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Counselor Mba first.

349
00:27:46,418 --> 00:27:48,139
[SPEAKER_01]: If you need me to repeat the question, I can.

350
00:27:48,199 --> 00:27:50,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are kind of long preambles.

351
00:27:50,399 --> 00:27:51,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Please do it.

352
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:51,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Do it.

353
00:27:52,100 --> 00:27:52,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Joe.

354
00:27:52,600 --> 00:27:52,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Good.

355
00:27:52,760 --> 00:27:59,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So really, which of the approaches would reduce housing costs most quickly, and what evidence supports your answer?

356
00:27:59,983 --> 00:28:02,764
[SPEAKER_01]: So in your case, you're saying rent stabilization.

357
00:28:03,324 --> 00:28:07,625
[SPEAKER_01]: In Mr. Oates' case, he's saying removing the zoning and permitting barriers.

358
00:28:08,225 --> 00:28:12,867
[SPEAKER_01]: And in Mr. Rousseau, he's saying substantial expansion of public housing.

359
00:28:13,887 --> 00:28:14,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no.

360
00:28:16,153 --> 00:28:21,682
[SPEAKER_00]: I wouldn't disagree with Mr. Oates and Mr. Russo.

361
00:28:21,802 --> 00:28:25,328
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we need a multi-pronged approach for this housing situation.

362
00:28:27,835 --> 00:28:38,401
[SPEAKER_00]: for mayor of this city on rent control, you know, even though they cannot, like, massage the change in more of itself into rent stabilization and all, I still believe in rent control as the only pathway forward.

363
00:28:38,841 --> 00:28:49,427
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, you know, we also need to, you know, embrace, you know, the YIMBY bill to remove barriers, you know, for this, you know, like, all this legislation that needs to build more housing.

364
00:28:49,807 --> 00:28:59,779
[SPEAKER_00]: We also need to encourage, yes, in God's backyard, that facilities should be able to build multifamily housing.

365
00:28:59,819 --> 00:29:04,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So there are multiple approaches to be able to put this housing forward.

366
00:29:04,564 --> 00:29:05,846
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like a silver bullet.

367
00:29:06,407 --> 00:29:10,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is my stand, and this is how I will approach this conversation.

368
00:29:10,869 --> 00:29:12,030
[SPEAKER_03]: Great, Chris Oates.

369
00:29:12,630 --> 00:29:21,395
[SPEAKER_03]: So the reason I led with housing production is that we see from Auckland, New Zealand to Austin, Texas, that the fastest way to drop rents is a large increase in supply.

370
00:29:21,675 --> 00:29:34,741
[SPEAKER_03]: But the nice thing when you do that is that you also then generate the revenue to pay for affordable housing, public housing, social housing, whatever you want to call it, non-market rate housing that is partially subsidized, usually by the government or by tax incentives.

371
00:29:35,042 --> 00:29:37,203
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you talk to anyone who works in affordable housing,

372
00:29:37,723 --> 00:29:40,364
[SPEAKER_03]: They will always tell you the big problem is where do they get the money?

373
00:29:40,524 --> 00:29:44,925
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it federal tax credits, which might be going away, affordable housing trust funds, anything else?

374
00:29:45,065 --> 00:29:47,366
[SPEAKER_03]: The question is always, where do we get the money?

375
00:29:47,486 --> 00:29:56,229
[SPEAKER_03]: So the reason why I emphasize production is that generates the revenue that you can then use to subsidize all the other types of non-market rate housing that you need.

376
00:29:56,449 --> 00:30:02,931
[SPEAKER_03]: And the other thing I should mention is that by bringing down the cost of construction through permitting reform, you then have

377
00:30:03,751 --> 00:30:09,834
[SPEAKER_03]: Cheaper homes that you can actually build, because now if it costs less to build that home, there are more projects that pencil out.

378
00:30:10,094 --> 00:30:14,256
[SPEAKER_03]: But more importantly, the subsidies for affordable housing go a lot further.

379
00:30:14,556 --> 00:30:19,138
[SPEAKER_03]: Here in Somerville, the Somerville Community Land Trust recently did a project, and it required between $607,000 of subsidies per unit.

380
00:30:21,919 --> 00:30:26,240
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, we don't have that much money to do those subsidies statewide.

381
00:30:26,320 --> 00:30:29,001
[SPEAKER_03]: We need 220,000 units across the state.

382
00:30:29,021 --> 00:30:32,202
[SPEAKER_03]: So if we did all of those at that rate, it would be $154 billion.

383
00:30:32,882 --> 00:30:34,002
[SPEAKER_03]: We don't have the money for that.

384
00:30:34,342 --> 00:30:41,284
[SPEAKER_03]: So by bringing down the cost of construction, making those subsidies go further, we have more people who need help getting the help that they need.

385
00:30:42,245 --> 00:30:42,565
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

386
00:30:43,045 --> 00:30:43,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Paul Rousseff.

387
00:30:44,933 --> 00:30:45,273
[SPEAKER_02]: All that.

388
00:30:46,854 --> 00:30:52,496
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, I actually think rent stabilization is probably the answer to the question of what will most quickly bring down the costs.

389
00:30:53,216 --> 00:31:01,759
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that one of the challenges is that, you know, we hear a lot about build, build, build, and my response to that is if the market was going to solve this problem, well,

390
00:31:02,767 --> 00:31:06,048
[SPEAKER_02]: The market's been around for a long time, and I haven't seen it solve the problem.

391
00:31:06,148 --> 00:31:07,548
[SPEAKER_02]: I only see the problem getting worse.

392
00:31:08,568 --> 00:31:16,770
[SPEAKER_02]: I think if we don't address the issue of decommodifying housing as an actual asset, which for anybody who owns a home, don't worry, it's not gonna go down.

393
00:31:17,110 --> 00:31:28,653
[SPEAKER_02]: If we could just have it go down to just maybe one or 2% increase a year, or even just inflation, that would be a dramatic improvement in the fact that housing is just a commodity, just an investment.

394
00:31:28,953 --> 00:31:31,494
[SPEAKER_02]: For a lot of people, it's their only thing for retirement.

395
00:31:33,274 --> 00:31:34,293
[SPEAKER_02]: that problem that

396
00:31:34,802 --> 00:31:43,183
[SPEAKER_02]: was invented, we're pretty good at inventing these things in our end-stage capitalist society, but it has to be directly addressed.

397
00:31:43,423 --> 00:31:58,486
[SPEAKER_02]: And one of the other things, the reason I said public housing and public-owned housing, social housing, housing that isn't just for folks at the bottom income scale, but folks who are, you know, not the folks at the top, they don't want it anyways, but folks that are in the middle income.

398
00:31:58,646 --> 00:32:04,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Frankly, folks that are now making six figures and can't possibly buy a home in Somerville.

399
00:32:04,647 --> 00:32:07,168
[SPEAKER_02]: ever, ever, like six figures.

400
00:32:07,268 --> 00:32:09,829
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's no hope for buying a home if you did not inherit it.

401
00:32:10,349 --> 00:32:14,651
[SPEAKER_02]: And this word I heard on a podcast called an inheritocracy, that's what we live in.

402
00:32:15,231 --> 00:32:16,552
[SPEAKER_02]: We live in an inheritocracy.

403
00:32:17,632 --> 00:32:18,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

404
00:32:18,372 --> 00:32:19,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So can I encapsulate?

405
00:32:22,014 --> 00:32:30,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Stabilize the market while continuing to build and make sure the public housing portion of that is included in everything that we do.

406
00:32:32,478 --> 00:32:33,058
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not running.

407
00:32:36,235 --> 00:32:36,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Question.

408
00:32:37,216 --> 00:32:46,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Somerville schools have repeatedly been under financial strain and the city has dedicated substantial funding to try to improve educational outcomes.

409
00:32:47,523 --> 00:32:49,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Rousseau, you have a solution.

410
00:32:50,466 --> 00:32:56,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Rewriting the Chapter 70 education aid formula and removing its inflation cap.

411
00:32:57,150 --> 00:33:00,013
[SPEAKER_01]: How much additional funding would your proposal require?

412
00:33:01,017 --> 00:33:08,921
[SPEAKER_01]: where would that money come from, and how would you persuade legislators representing communities that might receive a smaller share?

413
00:33:10,102 --> 00:33:13,244
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm going to ask you both a follow-up to that.

414
00:33:14,264 --> 00:33:19,127
[SPEAKER_02]: The idea that any community will receive a smaller share is probably not necessary.

415
00:33:20,447 --> 00:33:22,809
[SPEAKER_02]: We have a floor in our Chapter 70 formula.

416
00:33:23,409 --> 00:33:24,650
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't agree with it on principle.

417
00:33:24,910 --> 00:33:25,590
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a floor.

418
00:33:25,870 --> 00:33:28,292
[SPEAKER_02]: So if you're Weston, Wellesley,

419
00:33:29,052 --> 00:33:35,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Manchester-by-the-Sea, you get state aid for your students, even if you could easily afford to pay for it all.

420
00:33:36,495 --> 00:33:42,698
[SPEAKER_02]: And every one of those dollars is dollars that's not going to Somerville and Medford and lots of other communities that, frankly, need more.

421
00:33:43,739 --> 00:33:47,620
[SPEAKER_02]: The floor, I think, is something that should be removed from the Chapter 70 formula.

422
00:33:47,740 --> 00:33:50,842
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that is as likely to happen as climate

423
00:33:51,608 --> 00:33:52,709
[SPEAKER_02]: emergency will be over tomorrow.

424
00:33:54,111 --> 00:33:57,735
[SPEAKER_02]: But increasing the amount of money that is going to communities.

425
00:33:57,795 --> 00:34:01,299
[SPEAKER_02]: So there's this hold harmless part of the Chapter 70 formula.

426
00:34:01,519 --> 00:34:03,261
[SPEAKER_02]: This is really boring stuff if you don't know what it is.

427
00:34:03,601 --> 00:34:07,926
[SPEAKER_02]: It's how the state decides how much money to give cities and towns to pay for education.

428
00:34:10,447 --> 00:34:18,032
[SPEAKER_02]: When you are not technically supposed to get any more, they give you a token amount, it's called hold harmless, and it's per pupil.

429
00:34:18,353 --> 00:34:27,179
[SPEAKER_02]: So some years it's $20, some years it's $120, but if you can do math at all, you quickly realize that number is dramatically lower than inflation.

430
00:34:27,660 --> 00:34:34,325
[SPEAKER_02]: So for Somerville and Medford for the last decade, we get hold harmless, which means we get less money every year from the state.

431
00:34:34,865 --> 00:34:35,926
[SPEAKER_02]: if you include inflation.

432
00:34:36,347 --> 00:34:45,115
[SPEAKER_02]: And somehow we do that by not paving the roads or not hiring enough teachers or being inappropriate in negotiations and not giving appropriate raises.

433
00:34:46,276 --> 00:34:46,896
[SPEAKER_02]: And I'm out of time.

434
00:34:46,936 --> 00:34:47,117
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry.

435
00:34:47,177 --> 00:34:47,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Paul.

436
00:34:48,258 --> 00:34:50,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Mbarra and Chris Oates, let's do this.

437
00:34:50,900 --> 00:34:52,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you agree with that approach?

438
00:34:53,142 --> 00:34:55,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't, what's the difference?

439
00:34:56,786 --> 00:35:00,169
[SPEAKER_00]: With the approach that, you know, that Paul has described?

440
00:35:00,485 --> 00:35:23,096
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I mean, I appreciate, you know, like I think that it's whatever I think Paul is saying makes so much sense because you're even supposed to add the fact that, you know, there's also the evaluation is also based on property values, you know, and so and meanwhile, there are people in the city, Somerville and Medford, you know, they have their property values very high, but they are cash poor.

441
00:35:23,641 --> 00:35:30,563
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, there's like all these different evaluation that people, that it doesn't really match the reality of everyday, you know, like life.

442
00:35:30,583 --> 00:35:42,226
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I support, you know, like what Paul is saying that, you know, the state can do more, you know, to be able to like change this formula so that it is more just and equitable across municipalities.

443
00:35:42,788 --> 00:35:43,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

444
00:35:43,208 --> 00:35:43,448
[SPEAKER_03]: Chris?

445
00:35:43,908 --> 00:35:48,390
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, this is, yeah, this is going to be personal for me because my son's starting pre-K the day after the election.

446
00:35:48,730 --> 00:35:51,791
[SPEAKER_03]: So I will be a public school parent, but not until the election.

447
00:35:52,191 --> 00:36:01,675
[SPEAKER_03]: But I think if we're going to reopen the Chapter 70 funding formula, I think what we're likely to get into is not just what's going on today, but what is going to be happening over the next 15 years.

448
00:36:02,415 --> 00:36:07,319
[SPEAKER_03]: which is that with birth rates falling right now, student populations are going to be falling over the next generation.

449
00:36:07,359 --> 00:36:13,004
[SPEAKER_03]: So in 2050, it's projected that we'll have 19% fewer students in Massachusetts than what we have today.

450
00:36:13,244 --> 00:36:15,386
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's not going to be distributed evenly across the state.

451
00:36:15,446 --> 00:36:22,272
[SPEAKER_03]: So Somerville and Medford might be constant, but places in the Berkshires might have their student population go down by over a third.

452
00:36:22,612 --> 00:36:26,656
[SPEAKER_03]: And you cannot just heat the building a third less in winter if you have a third less kids.

453
00:36:27,036 --> 00:36:38,801
[SPEAKER_03]: So I think if we're going to be reopening, you know, the actual formula for funding to try to get more for Somerville and Medford, I think what we're likely to get into is a different debate driven by the representatives out in the Berkshires and in Worcester County.

454
00:36:39,381 --> 00:36:41,902
[SPEAKER_03]: What are we going to do with student falling populations?

455
00:36:41,922 --> 00:36:44,283
[SPEAKER_03]: Because a lot of education spending is per pupil.

456
00:36:44,743 --> 00:36:48,365
[SPEAKER_03]: So if they start getting less, you know, less money because they have fewer pupils,

457
00:36:49,065 --> 00:36:53,808
[SPEAKER_03]: they're gonna have difficult situations of are they laying off teachers, are they closing buildings, are they regionalizing?

458
00:36:54,149 --> 00:37:13,683
[SPEAKER_03]: And so while I'm not opposed to what Paul is saying, I'm saying if we're going to get into a big education funding debate, I think it's very likely to turn into what does Massachusetts need, not just right now, but what do all the different communities need in 2035, in 2040, in 2050, and that's probably gonna be ending up what the new formula is going to be.

459
00:37:14,283 --> 00:37:15,044
[SPEAKER_03]: Joe, can I respond to that?

460
00:37:15,344 --> 00:37:15,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

461
00:37:15,784 --> 00:37:16,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

462
00:37:16,124 --> 00:37:16,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Follow up.

463
00:37:17,225 --> 00:37:17,465
[SPEAKER_02]: I do.

464
00:37:18,265 --> 00:37:30,192
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you so much, Chris, because one of my main points was that Massachusetts does funding for education in a way that is actually, you know, if you live here and you've always lived here, you think that's how it goes.

465
00:37:30,552 --> 00:37:33,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Maryland does it by county, which is a much bigger unit.

466
00:37:33,614 --> 00:37:34,935
[SPEAKER_02]: Vermont does it by state.

467
00:37:35,215 --> 00:37:36,376
[SPEAKER_02]: That's obviously a bigger unit.

468
00:37:36,916 --> 00:37:43,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Our willingness to accept that you live one house over in Cambridge, you can have twice as much money spent on your education

469
00:37:44,035 --> 00:37:49,056
[SPEAKER_02]: than in Somerville, let's not talk about Medford, is a values problem.

470
00:37:50,436 --> 00:37:50,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

471
00:37:51,676 --> 00:37:52,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Next question.

472
00:37:52,657 --> 00:37:55,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Oates, you have never held elected office.

473
00:37:56,557 --> 00:38:03,879
[SPEAKER_01]: What experience do you have that Councilor Mba and Mr. Rousseau do not that voters should consider you?

474
00:38:04,779 --> 00:38:09,119
[SPEAKER_03]: So I worked, as I said, on the Rank Choice Voting campaign, 2018 through 2020.

475
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:11,780
[SPEAKER_03]: And so that was a statewide ballot initiative.

476
00:38:12,100 --> 00:38:13,061
[SPEAKER_03]: So we were doing two things.

477
00:38:13,141 --> 00:38:18,243
[SPEAKER_03]: First, we were trying to get a majority in the legislature because that is an easier way to pass it.

478
00:38:18,564 --> 00:38:25,708
[SPEAKER_03]: We were also trying to build the popular support for election reform without a real local example.

479
00:38:25,748 --> 00:38:29,069
[SPEAKER_03]: We were basically trying to set an example for the rest of the country.

480
00:38:29,570 --> 00:38:37,874
[SPEAKER_03]: When ranked choice voting had worked in the past, it was in Maine, they had a specific person, Paul LePage, the governor, very likely to be the next congressman from the second district, to point to.

481
00:38:37,914 --> 00:38:38,895
[SPEAKER_03]: So we had a harder job.

482
00:38:39,555 --> 00:38:51,518
[SPEAKER_03]: So that involved me going around to Democratic and Republican town committees across the state and trying to tell them why this issue, which might seem abstract and anodyne and kind of technical, was actually in their interest.

483
00:38:51,598 --> 00:38:53,478
[SPEAKER_03]: I had to talk with Libertarians and Greens.

484
00:38:53,518 --> 00:38:55,059
[SPEAKER_03]: I spoke with the League of Women Voters.

485
00:38:55,119 --> 00:38:59,360
[SPEAKER_03]: I sat in with legislators when their constituents would try to talk to them about it.

486
00:38:59,400 --> 00:39:01,780
[SPEAKER_03]: And I had to be the subject matter expert answering that.

487
00:39:02,160 --> 00:39:04,241
[SPEAKER_03]: And really, that is the job of a legislator.

488
00:39:04,281 --> 00:39:09,202
[SPEAKER_03]: If you want to pass legislation on Beacon Hill, you need a majority from across the state to be with you.

489
00:39:09,642 --> 00:39:13,904
[SPEAKER_03]: So my experience, I think, that is most relevant is the fact that I've already done that.

490
00:39:14,144 --> 00:39:15,565
[SPEAKER_03]: I've already tried to build that coalition.

491
00:39:15,605 --> 00:39:24,830
[SPEAKER_03]: I've learned what is it like to talk to someone from Worcester and say, yes, we care about this a lot in Somerville and Cambridge and Medford, but here's why you should care about it as well, even though you think it doesn't apply to you.

492
00:39:25,190 --> 00:39:27,431
[SPEAKER_03]: Here's why it helps your interests as well as ours.

493
00:39:27,912 --> 00:39:30,633
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if you want to get something passed, that's how you do it.

494
00:39:30,713 --> 00:39:33,995
[SPEAKER_03]: You make it so that it's their interest to help you out and to help the people here out.

495
00:39:34,635 --> 00:39:35,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

496
00:39:35,636 --> 00:39:40,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Will and Paul, you were once candidates who had never been elected yourselves.

497
00:39:40,882 --> 00:39:44,906
[SPEAKER_01]: You got anything you want to offer to Chris on first-time candidates?

498
00:39:49,477 --> 00:39:50,438
[SPEAKER_02]: I have, sure.

499
00:39:50,598 --> 00:39:51,439
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure, go ahead.

500
00:39:51,679 --> 00:39:56,204
[SPEAKER_02]: So I will say that I miss that being a first-time candidate.

501
00:39:56,324 --> 00:39:57,245
[SPEAKER_02]: I very much miss it.

502
00:39:58,126 --> 00:40:00,608
[SPEAKER_02]: My hope levels were different, really very high.

503
00:40:01,429 --> 00:40:09,157
[SPEAKER_02]: I will say that local city elections and governance is really such a different

504
00:40:11,084 --> 00:40:11,684
[SPEAKER_02]: It really is.

505
00:40:13,085 --> 00:40:16,108
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, there's 160 House members.

506
00:40:16,828 --> 00:40:24,455
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, I know all, I have every single Medford city council and school committee member on a group chat to say, you know, where are we going for dinner and, you know, or whatever.

507
00:40:25,415 --> 00:40:26,837
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not a thing at the State House.

508
00:40:27,537 --> 00:40:39,567
[SPEAKER_02]: And the thing that bothers me a lot about the State House is that a majority of the electeds can think we should pass a bill and they can co-sign a bill and it doesn't do a thing.

509
00:40:40,581 --> 00:40:46,163
[SPEAKER_02]: We, for seven years, we've had the menstrual products bill sitting and going nowhere.

510
00:40:46,764 --> 00:40:49,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Driver's license for homeless youth, ten years.

511
00:40:49,825 --> 00:40:51,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody would be opposed to it.

512
00:40:51,526 --> 00:40:51,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Nobody.

513
00:40:51,946 --> 00:40:53,667
[SPEAKER_02]: It costs nothing, but we can't do it.

514
00:40:53,887 --> 00:40:54,647
[SPEAKER_02]: It just can't happen.

515
00:40:55,347 --> 00:41:00,870
[SPEAKER_02]: The recess bill for kids, the Healthy Youth Act, all a decade, never get their day for a vote.

516
00:41:02,480 --> 00:41:09,123
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that addressing the machinery itself directly is the only way to really make any big changes.

517
00:41:09,323 --> 00:41:11,904
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think I am the guy that's going to change the State House.

518
00:41:12,424 --> 00:41:21,749
[SPEAKER_02]: Me and a coalition of three or four other folks who are attached at the hip and are unwilling to do anything other than talk about one issue at a time.

519
00:41:22,849 --> 00:41:23,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

520
00:41:23,830 --> 00:41:24,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Mbarra.

521
00:41:25,350 --> 00:41:25,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

522
00:41:25,871 --> 00:41:26,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Paul.

523
00:41:26,411 --> 00:41:28,992
[SPEAKER_00]: I was just, I mean, for me, all I have is just

524
00:41:30,547 --> 00:41:38,421
[SPEAKER_00]: deep respect for Mr. Oates because it takes a lot of courage you know to step up to run so uh that is why

525
00:41:39,160 --> 00:41:45,683
[SPEAKER_00]: When I made my introduction, I appreciated, you know, your leadership and Jack and the Dems for putting this together.

526
00:41:45,723 --> 00:41:50,485
[SPEAKER_00]: And I said, Mr. Oswald, be happy because this is where, you know, you can see the contrast.

527
00:41:50,565 --> 00:41:54,987
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, we can hear what you stand for, what we stand for, what everybody stands for.

528
00:41:55,048 --> 00:41:56,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is, for me, this is huge.

529
00:41:57,068 --> 00:41:58,069
[SPEAKER_00]: So that is all, man.

530
00:41:58,109 --> 00:41:59,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Congratulations for stepping up.

531
00:42:00,430 --> 00:42:00,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Will.

532
00:42:01,671 --> 00:42:04,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Something tells me I'm not going to get them to argue.

533
00:42:05,452 --> 00:42:07,253
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying my best here, folks.

534
00:42:07,914 --> 00:42:08,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Next question.

535
00:42:09,934 --> 00:42:16,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Mba, you have stated that if elected, you will step down as a Somerville City Councilor.

536
00:42:17,756 --> 00:42:24,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Russo, you have said that you would continue working at Dana-Farber while serving in the legislature.

537
00:42:24,638 --> 00:42:29,419
[SPEAKER_01]: To each of you, would you intend to serve as a full-time state representative?

538
00:42:34,241 --> 00:42:37,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go to Councilor Mba.

539
00:42:37,242 --> 00:42:38,342
[SPEAKER_01]: You are going to give up?

540
00:42:39,030 --> 00:42:39,430
[SPEAKER_01]: your seat.

541
00:42:39,591 --> 00:42:42,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, and here was my methodology.

542
00:42:42,974 --> 00:42:51,383
[SPEAKER_00]: When I started collecting signatures, again, people, my supporters, fans, they're like, oh, well, are you going to keep your seat?

543
00:42:52,003 --> 00:42:53,505
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm like, I haven't even thought about it.

544
00:42:53,645 --> 00:42:56,468
[SPEAKER_00]: They're like, oh, we want you to finish your term.

545
00:42:56,508 --> 00:42:57,929
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm like, sure, I'll finish my term.

546
00:42:58,450 --> 00:42:59,951
[SPEAKER_00]: So then they started getting excited.

547
00:43:00,977 --> 00:43:07,422
[SPEAKER_00]: when after I've submitted my papers and everything, like the majority of people that signed my nomination paper, they wanted me to finish my term.

548
00:43:07,442 --> 00:43:13,346
[SPEAKER_00]: But when I started looking at reality, some people approached me in good faith and said like, well, I think it might be too much.

549
00:43:13,406 --> 00:43:17,909
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I'm like, wow, I think also I have never been in that office before.

550
00:43:17,949 --> 00:43:23,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to respect the city of Medford and the city of Somerville, I think it is in my interest

551
00:43:24,053 --> 00:43:31,161
[SPEAKER_00]: to resign so that I can fully focus on both districts and serve them with the skills and ability that I've been entrusted with.

552
00:43:32,301 --> 00:43:32,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Rousseau.

553
00:43:33,402 --> 00:43:33,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

554
00:43:34,783 --> 00:43:38,185
[SPEAKER_02]: So I don't see this as choosing between two forms of public service.

555
00:43:39,025 --> 00:43:43,288
[SPEAKER_02]: My work at the Dana-Farber supports technology behind cancer research, and I am proud of it.

556
00:43:44,409 --> 00:43:49,452
[SPEAKER_02]: I've also spent nine years on the school committee while working, raising a family, negotiating contracts.

557
00:43:49,552 --> 00:44:00,139
[SPEAKER_02]: I've been at the administrative offices at least four days every single week for probably the last seven years or so, all during the day while I'm allegedly working.

558
00:44:00,339 --> 00:44:01,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't tell my boss.

559
00:44:03,178 --> 00:44:12,066
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I do not want to walk away from my work that matters, but I also know that when I take on a responsibility, I organize my life around it.

560
00:44:13,667 --> 00:44:16,790
[SPEAKER_02]: I could not have been on the school committee and done the work I do.

561
00:44:18,180 --> 00:44:20,762
[SPEAKER_02]: if I had a nine to five job that required me to be there.

562
00:44:21,823 --> 00:44:24,525
[SPEAKER_02]: I wouldn't have done it because that's not who I am.

563
00:44:24,885 --> 00:44:27,887
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever time is required is the time that a project gets.

564
00:44:28,988 --> 00:44:30,989
[SPEAKER_02]: So my schedule will be fine.

565
00:44:31,409 --> 00:44:32,470
[SPEAKER_02]: My boss is fine.

566
00:44:33,150 --> 00:44:35,192
[SPEAKER_02]: As he says, we're never waiting on you.

567
00:44:35,232 --> 00:44:36,873
[SPEAKER_02]: You're always sitting around waiting for us.

568
00:44:37,734 --> 00:44:38,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Different problem.

569
00:44:39,415 --> 00:44:41,656
[SPEAKER_02]: But the real question is not whether I have another job.

570
00:44:42,457 --> 00:44:46,900
[SPEAKER_02]: It is whether I show up, prepare, remain accessible, and deliver.

571
00:44:47,365 --> 00:44:48,420
[SPEAKER_02]: And I have a record for doing that.

572
00:44:50,877 --> 00:44:51,538
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you very much.

573
00:44:51,618 --> 00:44:52,178
[SPEAKER_03]: Mr. Oates.

574
00:44:52,618 --> 00:44:59,324
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, I said from the beginning that this is going to be my full-time job, because that's what you deserve, and that is what is required to actually do the job well.

575
00:44:59,804 --> 00:45:11,914
[SPEAKER_03]: I know a lot of folks who've worked in and around Beacon Hill, and they can tell you that the legislature might be transparent to the outside, but those on the inside know who are the workers and those who are getting reelected but maybe not showing up all the time.

576
00:45:11,995 --> 00:45:18,040
[SPEAKER_03]: Because ultimately, if you want to get something passed, it's not that it gets passed in the budget in July and you start working on it in July.

577
00:45:18,660 --> 00:45:20,381
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm going to start working on September 3rd.

578
00:45:20,401 --> 00:45:31,224
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm going to give myself one day off, but start working on September 3rd to get my entire legislative agenda for the first term done by December, know who I'm working with, what is the actual legislation we're firing, what is the strategy.

579
00:45:31,544 --> 00:45:45,209
[SPEAKER_03]: Of course you're going to have to adapt when the session hits, but I would much rather be adapting from a place of preparedness and knowing that all of my effort is going into representing this district and having the meetings on the issues that might not even seem at the time like they're worth my time.

580
00:45:45,509 --> 00:45:47,670
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, it might be, oh, we're going to have a meeting on this thing.

581
00:45:48,071 --> 00:45:50,052
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know if it's really worth it, but I'm going to do it anyway.

582
00:45:50,072 --> 00:45:57,157
[SPEAKER_03]: Because those are often the ones where you discover the cracks in the system, the things where people are falling through, where you can actually really make a difference.

583
00:45:57,217 --> 00:46:06,543
[SPEAKER_03]: So I know a lot of people who have worked in and around Beacon Hill, as I said, and they tell me that there's really no question, if you want to do the job well, if you want to do the work,

584
00:46:07,223 --> 00:46:08,584
[SPEAKER_03]: You really need to be putting in the time.

585
00:46:08,844 --> 00:46:11,947
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's why I've said from the beginning, this will be my full-time job.

586
00:46:11,987 --> 00:46:14,229
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm going to give up my company to someone else.

587
00:46:14,509 --> 00:46:18,313
[SPEAKER_03]: And I will have nothing else other than this and child care, obviously.

588
00:46:18,793 --> 00:46:19,433
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you very much.

589
00:46:19,474 --> 00:46:20,454
[SPEAKER_03]: Joe, can I add on to that?

590
00:46:21,175 --> 00:46:21,335
[SPEAKER_02]: Sure.

591
00:46:21,355 --> 00:46:24,117
[SPEAKER_02]: So a legislator makes $82,000 a year.

592
00:46:24,478 --> 00:46:29,182
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you live in Medford or Somerville, and you can live on $82,000 a year, you already own your home.

593
00:46:34,226 --> 00:46:41,368
[SPEAKER_02]: The legislature's methodology for providing pressure is to give you committees where you can make a lot more money.

594
00:46:42,169 --> 00:46:46,810
[SPEAKER_02]: And frankly, if you don't need that money, then you already come from enough wealth where it doesn't matter.

595
00:46:47,931 --> 00:46:53,212
[SPEAKER_02]: But for a lot of regular folks, and I count myself along that line, I can't live on $82,000 a year.

596
00:46:53,432 --> 00:46:54,593
[SPEAKER_02]: I would have to live in my car.

597
00:46:55,353 --> 00:46:55,633
[SPEAKER_02]: So.

598
00:46:58,234 --> 00:46:58,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

599
00:46:58,934 --> 00:47:00,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Do we have a timer problem or?

600
00:47:00,875 --> 00:47:01,495
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know what that is.

601
00:47:03,110 --> 00:47:05,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, he lowered the volume on us.

602
00:47:05,151 --> 00:47:05,371
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

603
00:47:06,431 --> 00:47:07,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

604
00:47:07,512 --> 00:47:07,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Are we done?

605
00:47:07,992 --> 00:47:08,652
[SPEAKER_01]: We're done with that.

606
00:47:08,953 --> 00:47:10,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go to the next question.

607
00:47:10,173 --> 00:47:15,295
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm going to kind of eliminate follow-ups for the next couple of questions, because we're running a little bit behind.

608
00:47:15,915 --> 00:47:21,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Rousseau, you support establishing a state single-payer health care system.

609
00:47:22,238 --> 00:47:31,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Oates has said he doubts that single-payer health care is workable at the state level, more so with federal cuts

610
00:47:32,523 --> 00:47:36,865
[SPEAKER_01]: more so with federal cuts making state budgets even tighter.

611
00:47:37,866 --> 00:47:44,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Rousseau, how would you finance single payer, especially without federal cooperation?

612
00:47:45,549 --> 00:47:56,134
[SPEAKER_01]: And Mr. Oates, if state single payer is unworkable, please explain your alternative for improving health care costs and access in Massachusetts.

613
00:47:57,255 --> 00:48:00,096
[SPEAKER_02]: So paying for it is not actually a challenge.

614
00:48:00,416 --> 00:48:01,397
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only a challenge if

615
00:48:02,948 --> 00:48:05,069
[SPEAKER_02]: if you're really, really, really rich.

616
00:48:05,550 --> 00:48:09,332
[SPEAKER_02]: We have the millionaire's tax, 4% on every dollar you earn over a million.

617
00:48:09,552 --> 00:48:15,376
[SPEAKER_02]: So the first, you know, the first million, you're not paying any more than you, me, or what I assume is most of the people in the room.

618
00:48:16,177 --> 00:48:20,720
[SPEAKER_02]: After that, the first million, you start paying 4 cents additional per dollar.

619
00:48:21,820 --> 00:48:25,142
[SPEAKER_02]: I propose that for every additional million, we add 1% more.

620
00:48:26,374 --> 00:48:27,375
[SPEAKER_02]: up to 12%.

621
00:48:27,935 --> 00:48:33,659
[SPEAKER_02]: There are people in Massachusetts, and I don't mean one, whose income last year was over $50 million.

622
00:48:33,919 --> 00:48:38,882
[SPEAKER_02]: And don't forget, these are folks with a lot of lawyers and CPAs that can figure out how to not pay taxes.

623
00:48:39,262 --> 00:48:42,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Their actual taxable income was over $50 million last year.

624
00:48:43,769 --> 00:48:49,711
[SPEAKER_02]: So I'm going to struggle to cry if we add another 1% or if you are up that high, 12%.

625
00:48:51,131 --> 00:48:54,692
[SPEAKER_02]: That would generate $8.7 billion a year.

626
00:48:55,672 --> 00:49:04,335
[SPEAKER_02]: The reason you can't go over 12% is you end up with a situation where they then stop taking their income because people in that kind of an income bracket can just decide to not take income.

627
00:49:05,015 --> 00:49:08,476
[SPEAKER_02]: So we don't want to go too high, although in my heart I would like to go higher.

628
00:49:09,476 --> 00:49:11,697
[SPEAKER_02]: But 8.7 billion is a lot of money.

629
00:49:12,317 --> 00:49:16,259
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think we could do an awful lot for Medicare for All and probably a lot of other things.

630
00:49:18,080 --> 00:49:19,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Chris Oates, why is it unworkable?

631
00:49:20,261 --> 00:49:22,682
[SPEAKER_03]: So I've lived under a single-payer health care system.

632
00:49:22,702 --> 00:49:23,562
[SPEAKER_03]: I lived in England.

633
00:49:23,822 --> 00:49:26,963
[SPEAKER_03]: And I remember when I showed up, they said, oh, by the way, here's your doctor.

634
00:49:27,043 --> 00:49:28,504
[SPEAKER_03]: And I was like, OK, what's the co-pay?

635
00:49:28,544 --> 00:49:29,344
[SPEAKER_03]: What's the insurance?

636
00:49:29,364 --> 00:49:30,465
[SPEAKER_03]: And they're like, what are you talking about?

637
00:49:30,485 --> 00:49:31,385
[SPEAKER_03]: You just go to the doctor.

638
00:49:31,745 --> 00:49:32,426
[SPEAKER_03]: Just go to the doctor.

639
00:49:32,486 --> 00:49:33,526
[SPEAKER_03]: And it was great.

640
00:49:33,586 --> 00:49:34,827
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, it's not utopia.

641
00:49:35,907 --> 00:49:40,450
[SPEAKER_03]: problems with wait times, they have problems with how much they pay their staff, but it is a better system.

642
00:49:40,490 --> 00:49:43,491
[SPEAKER_03]: So if we could get Medicare for all, I would be very happy.

643
00:49:43,712 --> 00:49:47,754
[SPEAKER_03]: And if we could have a budget that could make that work, I would be very happy.

644
00:49:47,774 --> 00:49:48,715
[SPEAKER_03]: I would gladly pay for it.

645
00:49:49,135 --> 00:49:54,258
[SPEAKER_03]: But the fact is that our healthcare system is split between the state and the federal government.

646
00:49:55,058 --> 00:49:56,678
[SPEAKER_03]: and the federal government can run deficits.

647
00:49:56,919 --> 00:50:00,639
[SPEAKER_03]: So if healthcare costs go up, the federal government just prints more debt.

648
00:50:00,919 --> 00:50:07,741
[SPEAKER_03]: Massachusetts cannot run deficits, meaning if healthcare costs rise more than expected, that money has to come out of somewhere else.

649
00:50:07,801 --> 00:50:11,862
[SPEAKER_03]: We might be cutting teacher salaries, cutting the MBTA, cutting other things.

650
00:50:11,942 --> 00:50:16,843
[SPEAKER_03]: And we've already seen this in Boston, where they had to lay off teachers in part because healthcare costs were rising.

651
00:50:16,923 --> 00:50:19,264
[SPEAKER_03]: So I am not against single-payer healthcare.

652
00:50:19,284 --> 00:50:20,504
[SPEAKER_03]: I want to be very clear.

653
00:50:21,024 --> 00:50:24,445
[SPEAKER_03]: If there is a plan that we can do it, and we can do it with enough of a buffer

654
00:50:24,905 --> 00:50:28,007
[SPEAKER_03]: so that we're not putting everything else in the state at risk every single year.

655
00:50:28,747 --> 00:50:29,828
[SPEAKER_03]: I'd be happy to sign on to it.

656
00:50:30,388 --> 00:50:36,452
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, I don't want to promise something knowing that we may do it and then it may not work out.

657
00:50:37,152 --> 00:50:41,054
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is specifically because of the state-federal balance of who can run a deficit.

658
00:50:41,335 --> 00:50:42,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

659
00:50:42,175 --> 00:50:43,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Mba, which one is right?

660
00:50:44,637 --> 00:50:45,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Mr. Russo.

661
00:50:46,277 --> 00:50:47,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Mr. Russo, plus one.

662
00:50:48,339 --> 00:50:54,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, as somebody who, you know, came to this country and struggled with health insurance, like I mentioned in the beginning,

663
00:50:54,537 --> 00:50:56,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And I lived in Sweden for three years.

664
00:50:56,618 --> 00:50:58,919
[SPEAKER_00]: I never saw a single bill, just went to the hospital.

665
00:50:59,359 --> 00:51:00,239
[SPEAKER_00]: We can do this stuff.

666
00:51:00,720 --> 00:51:01,740
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the political will.

667
00:51:02,000 --> 00:51:05,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Look how much money we spend in war, bombing people across the world.

668
00:51:05,982 --> 00:51:08,663
[SPEAKER_00]: We can use just a fraction of that money.

669
00:51:08,863 --> 00:51:14,065
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, just to plus one again to Mr. Rosa, that talk about the millionaire tax.

670
00:51:14,265 --> 00:51:15,025
[SPEAKER_00]: He's done so much.

671
00:51:17,126 --> 00:51:19,027
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, you know, this is like a no-brainer.

672
00:51:19,388 --> 00:51:24,190
[SPEAKER_00]: We just need the political will and the drive to push, you know, for this, you know, policies to happen.

673
00:51:24,230 --> 00:51:24,790
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thank you.

674
00:51:25,831 --> 00:51:26,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

675
00:51:27,032 --> 00:51:27,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Next question.

676
00:51:30,613 --> 00:51:36,697
[SPEAKER_01]: This next question is about the Commonwealth Stabilization Fund, also known as the Rainy Day Fund.

677
00:51:37,377 --> 00:51:42,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Hopefully, all of us have a little rainy day fund socked away as individuals, but some don't.

678
00:51:44,227 --> 00:51:53,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So this question, Mr. Rousseau, you've advocated using part of that fund to prevent cuts resulting from reductions in federal assistance.

679
00:51:54,615 --> 00:52:01,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Oates, you have expressed some reluctance to use the Commonwealth's Rainy Day Fund outside a recession.

680
00:52:02,942 --> 00:52:11,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So Mr. Rousseau, should we dig into the Rainy Day Fund to protect Massachusetts residents from the effects of the Trump administration's budget cuts?

681
00:52:12,459 --> 00:52:16,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, I'll say more than that.

682
00:52:16,962 --> 00:52:30,170
[SPEAKER_02]: If we end up with Trump for the next 25 years, which I can't fathom how he would live that long, but if Trump really just never goes away and we don't have another election, frankly, I think this is not one of our biggest problems.

683
00:52:31,731 --> 00:52:41,498
[SPEAKER_02]: If, in fact, three years from now, we have a somehow sane administration, then I imagine that these kinds of cuts will not stand.

684
00:52:42,572 --> 00:52:51,056
[SPEAKER_02]: We can afford three years of another $600 million coming out of our rainy day fund so that we don't have to kick all these people off of mass health.

685
00:52:52,497 --> 00:53:05,203
[SPEAKER_02]: If it was a permanent problem where there was no future where we could imagine this wasn't going to happen, where we might someday stop having to have this huge hole, then I think we should have a different conversation.

686
00:53:07,390 --> 00:53:09,972
[SPEAKER_02]: We're not talking about 20 years of having to keep this up.

687
00:53:10,352 --> 00:53:12,453
[SPEAKER_02]: In my mind, we're talking about three years.

688
00:53:13,374 --> 00:53:20,298
[SPEAKER_02]: And then if the federal government is in fact no different than it is today, we have a lot of other bigger problems to solve.

689
00:53:20,338 --> 00:53:26,062
[SPEAKER_02]: And obviously, I don't think the entire Rainy Day Fund should just be used to plug the MassHealth hole.

690
00:53:27,362 --> 00:53:31,305
[SPEAKER_02]: But $600 million, it's a lot for the budget of MassHealth.

691
00:53:32,292 --> 00:53:35,453
[SPEAKER_02]: but it's not that big a chunk of the rainy day fund.

692
00:53:35,533 --> 00:53:37,254
[SPEAKER_02]: So yeah, when is it raining?

693
00:53:37,394 --> 00:53:40,575
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't understand when our government thinks it's raining.

694
00:53:41,256 --> 00:53:43,096
[SPEAKER_02]: It's freaking pouring out.

695
00:53:44,377 --> 00:53:45,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

696
00:53:45,037 --> 00:53:49,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Mr. Oates, is that a responsible way to use the rainy day fund?

697
00:53:50,059 --> 00:53:50,480
[SPEAKER_03]: It can be.

698
00:53:50,720 --> 00:53:59,489
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, I think it comes down to the numbers and what it is like we're looking at, because if we get elected, it is going to be July of 2027 when our first budget is coming in.

699
00:54:00,149 --> 00:54:08,637
[SPEAKER_03]: And one of the problems that we had during the Great Recession of 07 to 09 is that a lot of state governments, again, because we can't run a deficit, had to cut

700
00:54:09,338 --> 00:54:16,583
[SPEAKER_03]: We had to take out teachers and nurses, police officers, a lot of other positions, which not only hurts the economy, but really affects the people involved.

701
00:54:17,024 --> 00:54:24,509
[SPEAKER_03]: So one of the reasons we did that is because rainy day funds across the country got depleted or they were not seen to be large enough.

702
00:54:24,910 --> 00:54:27,692
[SPEAKER_03]: So if we have a situation next year when the budget is coming up,

703
00:54:28,312 --> 00:54:36,935
[SPEAKER_03]: where it looks as if we can afford it and we're not going into recession where we are going to need that rainy day fund for every other thing that the government does, sure.

704
00:54:37,515 --> 00:54:41,977
[SPEAKER_03]: But I remember when we filled this out, it was like March or April of this year.

705
00:54:42,637 --> 00:54:47,999
[SPEAKER_03]: And we were not, you know, I cannot promise what the economy is going to be 14 months in the future.

706
00:54:48,099 --> 00:54:49,500
[SPEAKER_03]: And I don't think it's responsible to say

707
00:54:50,080 --> 00:54:56,683
[SPEAKER_03]: we will spend a certain amount of money on a certain thing when that money is supposed to be for everyone in the event of a recession.

708
00:54:56,783 --> 00:54:59,564
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, yes, if we're not in a recession, that's a different story.

709
00:54:59,904 --> 00:55:11,029
[SPEAKER_03]: But if we are, that is what it is there for, so that our teachers and our hospitals do not go under, again, because Massachusetts can't run a deficit and we don't know if the federal government will come in and bail us out as they did during COVID.

710
00:55:11,529 --> 00:55:12,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

711
00:55:12,690 --> 00:55:17,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Imba, you've had to make these kind of decisions on Somerville City Council.

712
00:55:19,097 --> 00:55:21,498
[SPEAKER_01]: you've utilized stabilization funds in the past.

713
00:55:22,218 --> 00:55:23,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you agree with the approach here?

714
00:55:23,599 --> 00:55:28,360
[SPEAKER_00]: 100% again to Mr. Russo.

715
00:55:29,001 --> 00:55:34,443
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, so on the city council, we have all these various stabilization accounts.

716
00:55:34,663 --> 00:55:36,864
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like almost like 55 of them.

717
00:55:37,324 --> 00:55:40,305
[SPEAKER_00]: If you add all that money, you know, like it's probably not of

718
00:55:40,976 --> 00:55:42,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Mr. Marshall Bone is right there.

719
00:55:42,577 --> 00:55:45,218
[SPEAKER_00]: This is our finance director.

720
00:55:45,258 --> 00:55:46,659
[SPEAKER_00]: This is my number one thing.

721
00:55:46,799 --> 00:55:47,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Follow the money.

722
00:55:48,080 --> 00:55:55,924
[SPEAKER_00]: And I instructed the administration to consolidate all this money, because when IKEA was coming to Somerville, they would give like $500,000, and IKEA never came.

723
00:55:58,225 --> 00:55:59,145
[SPEAKER_00]: is sitting somewhere.

724
00:55:59,245 --> 00:56:01,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Another company will come.

725
00:56:01,126 --> 00:56:02,226
[SPEAKER_00]: They will put some money.

726
00:56:02,606 --> 00:56:03,926
[SPEAKER_00]: And this money, they're all sitting there.

727
00:56:03,966 --> 00:56:05,366
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, consolidate this money.

728
00:56:05,426 --> 00:56:09,167
[SPEAKER_00]: We can repurpose it, legally possible, and help our constituents.

729
00:56:09,507 --> 00:56:16,988
[SPEAKER_00]: And that money, we've created a community stabilization account that has been helping, food for free, all these different needs that we have in the community.

730
00:56:17,008 --> 00:56:18,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So yes, it is pouring.

731
00:56:19,209 --> 00:56:20,749
[SPEAKER_00]: We are in a rainy emergency.

732
00:56:20,769 --> 00:56:22,109
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if that is even a word.

733
00:56:22,569 --> 00:56:27,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So we need to use that rainy day money to be able to help stabilize.

734
00:56:27,970 --> 00:56:28,651
[SPEAKER_00]: the community.

735
00:56:29,472 --> 00:56:31,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Still got some time on the clock there councilor you want it?

736
00:56:32,556 --> 00:56:42,548
[SPEAKER_00]: No again it's just I'm passionate about you know these kind of like issues and so and we've led on that that's why I'm talking about I bring my experience I have a track record

737
00:56:42,881 --> 00:56:48,384
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the good and bad, is that there's nothing to figure me out.

738
00:56:48,744 --> 00:56:51,486
[SPEAKER_00]: I have led the city for nearly a decade.

739
00:56:51,526 --> 00:56:54,768
[SPEAKER_00]: So there's nothing, that's why you go on my website, I only have my record.

740
00:56:54,788 --> 00:56:56,589
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have all these good ideas.

741
00:56:56,969 --> 00:56:59,491
[SPEAKER_00]: I wanna do X, Y, Z. Thank you very much.

742
00:56:59,511 --> 00:57:01,092
[SPEAKER_01]: We're gonna look to our timer.

743
00:57:01,192 --> 00:57:03,053
[SPEAKER_01]: We got time for closing statements.

744
00:57:03,742 --> 00:57:04,663
[SPEAKER_01]: We sure do.

745
00:57:05,284 --> 00:57:10,408
[SPEAKER_01]: So the closing statements from each of the candidates for the 34th Middlesex District.

746
00:57:10,789 --> 00:57:16,634
[SPEAKER_01]: We're going to start with, how about, let's go to Mr. Rousseau first.

747
00:57:16,794 --> 00:57:17,695
[SPEAKER_02]: All right, it's me.

748
00:57:19,597 --> 00:57:23,438
[SPEAKER_02]: When I was a work-study student in college, I discovered computer programming.

749
00:57:23,458 --> 00:57:24,739
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't discover it.

750
00:57:24,879 --> 00:57:30,800
[SPEAKER_02]: I discovered that it existed, which turned into a passion for problem-solving and then into a career.

751
00:57:31,300 --> 00:57:32,021
[SPEAKER_02]: And I was lucky.

752
00:57:32,541 --> 00:57:35,722
[SPEAKER_02]: But chance alone shouldn't decide who builds a life of meaning.

753
00:57:36,522 --> 00:57:41,144
[SPEAKER_02]: Years later, I co-led a programming club at my kids' elementary school.

754
00:57:41,725 --> 00:57:46,047
[SPEAKER_02]: And when I asked the question, what does the school offer afterwards?

755
00:57:46,147 --> 00:57:47,968
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the kids were so excited.

756
00:57:48,448 --> 00:57:51,370
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you know anything about computer science, it's mostly men.

757
00:57:52,050 --> 00:57:55,132
[SPEAKER_02]: And frankly, the girls were the ones that were shining in the class.

758
00:57:55,292 --> 00:57:56,472
[SPEAKER_02]: So I was very excited.

759
00:57:57,533 --> 00:57:59,474
[SPEAKER_02]: And so I asked what was next.

760
00:57:59,674 --> 00:58:00,855
[SPEAKER_02]: And the answer was, there's nothing.

761
00:58:00,895 --> 00:58:01,955
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no curriculum.

762
00:58:02,215 --> 00:58:03,736
[SPEAKER_02]: I was very dismayed by the answer.

763
00:58:04,576 --> 00:58:05,537
[SPEAKER_02]: Then came the election.

764
00:58:06,148 --> 00:58:07,569
[SPEAKER_02]: the election of 2016.

765
00:58:08,769 --> 00:58:13,931
[SPEAKER_02]: And when my kids went to bed, we all knew Hillary Clinton was going to be the president.

766
00:58:14,972 --> 00:58:22,395
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I went upstairs in the morning to wake my daughter, who was nine, and I told her who was going to be the next president.

767
00:58:22,655 --> 00:58:29,217
[SPEAKER_02]: And she opened her eyes, just so cute, and they were just barely open, and she said, well, shit.

768
00:58:31,730 --> 00:58:37,756
[SPEAKER_02]: I was very proud as a parent to know that that's the right way to use those words, even though I wouldn't like you to use them.

769
00:58:38,897 --> 00:58:40,839
[SPEAKER_02]: And then we talked about what we could do next.

770
00:58:40,899 --> 00:58:45,324
[SPEAKER_02]: So instead of doing nothing, I ran for office, and I won by 28 votes.

771
00:58:45,424 --> 00:58:48,767
[SPEAKER_02]: And the incumbent, longtime incumbent, wanted a recount, so I won by 32 votes.

772
00:58:48,787 --> 00:58:49,868
[SPEAKER_02]: I had to pay $1,000 for each of those votes.

773
00:58:52,971 --> 00:59:07,542
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I have a real passion for changing things because my own experience was a government that failed, but that experience tells me all the things that a government could be doing and it's not.

774
00:59:08,263 --> 00:59:17,550
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I really am looking forward to working with the different agencies and working with everyone, frankly, because that's, you know, I'm in the Medford Public Schools, I'm working with everyone.

775
00:59:18,171 --> 00:59:20,913
[SPEAKER_02]: And I look forward to serving you and being your

776
00:59:22,214 --> 00:59:22,894
[SPEAKER_02]: working for you.

777
00:59:23,574 --> 00:59:23,895
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you.

778
00:59:24,315 --> 00:59:24,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Paul.

779
00:59:25,135 --> 00:59:26,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's go to Councilor Mba.

780
00:59:28,897 --> 00:59:29,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Joe.

781
00:59:29,898 --> 00:59:34,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And to everybody here, thank you to my notable candidates and colleagues.

782
00:59:36,142 --> 00:59:38,363
[SPEAKER_00]: First of all, I have a suit and a tie.

783
00:59:38,503 --> 00:59:42,765
[SPEAKER_00]: I bike, yes, and it was warm, so that's why I don't look this spiffy.

784
00:59:43,526 --> 00:59:46,908
[SPEAKER_00]: So we've had two different perspectives about the future of our district.

785
00:59:47,868 --> 00:59:49,649
[SPEAKER_00]: And I have one simple question for you all.

786
00:59:50,290 --> 00:59:53,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is prepared to get results on day one?

787
00:59:54,293 --> 00:59:56,934
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, this district deserves more than good intentions.

788
00:59:57,835 --> 00:59:59,316
[SPEAKER_00]: It deserves proven leadership.

789
01:00:00,237 --> 01:00:10,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And throughout my life, from immigrating to this country, to raising my family here, to serving on the city council, I have never forgotten why I entered public service.

790
01:00:11,665 --> 01:00:14,867
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe every family deserves an affordable home.

791
01:00:16,126 --> 01:00:18,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Every worker deserves dignity and fair wages.

792
01:00:19,507 --> 01:00:21,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Every child deserves a great public education.

793
01:00:22,548 --> 01:00:24,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Every senior deserves security.

794
01:00:25,288 --> 01:00:30,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Every neighborhood deserves safe streets, clean parks, and a reliable public transportation.

795
01:00:31,570 --> 01:00:37,691
[SPEAKER_00]: And every resident deserves a representative who listens, who shows up, and who gets things done.

796
01:00:38,592 --> 01:00:39,192
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm that person.

797
01:00:40,200 --> 01:00:42,803
[SPEAKER_00]: And I respectfully ask for your vote on September 1st.

798
01:00:43,164 --> 01:00:44,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you for the opportunity.

799
01:00:44,485 --> 01:00:45,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Will and Bar.

800
01:00:46,027 --> 01:00:46,628
[SPEAKER_03]: Chris Oates?

801
01:00:46,928 --> 01:00:48,350
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, thank you very much for being here.

802
01:00:48,390 --> 01:00:49,031
[SPEAKER_03]: It has been great.

803
01:00:49,071 --> 01:00:49,872
[SPEAKER_03]: And yes, Will is right.

804
01:00:49,912 --> 01:00:54,477
[SPEAKER_03]: I have been asking for debates because you deserve to be able to choose who your next representative is.

805
01:00:54,978 --> 01:00:58,101
[SPEAKER_03]: As I said at the beginning, we live in the least competitive state legislature in the country.

806
01:00:59,002 --> 01:01:02,485
[SPEAKER_03]: And we deserve to be the ones making those choices and making real choices.

807
01:01:03,626 --> 01:01:06,669
[SPEAKER_03]: I think I alluded to it, but I've lived kind of surrounded by medicine my whole life.

808
01:01:07,069 --> 01:01:10,132
[SPEAKER_03]: My dad's a doctor, my wife's a doctor, her dad's a doctor, her sister's a doctor.

809
01:01:10,572 --> 01:01:13,294
[SPEAKER_03]: And medicine is pretty simple when it comes down to it.

810
01:01:13,314 --> 01:01:17,478
[SPEAKER_03]: A patient comes in, you listen to them, you diagnose the problem, you treat them.

811
01:01:17,858 --> 01:01:19,939
[SPEAKER_03]: And I think government should basically be the same way.

812
01:01:19,979 --> 01:01:26,683
[SPEAKER_03]: Your representative should listen to you, work with advocates and activists and experts to try to diagnose the problem, and then actually solve them.

813
01:01:26,903 --> 01:01:28,764
[SPEAKER_03]: And you need to actually solve them.

814
01:01:28,804 --> 01:01:32,806
[SPEAKER_03]: You need to deal with the reality of what your situation is.

815
01:01:33,366 --> 01:01:39,950
[SPEAKER_03]: If you're at the statehouse and you need to get a majority of the legislature on your side, you need to meet them where they are and bring them to your side.

816
01:01:40,450 --> 01:01:49,254
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, no one has ever said of my doctor, oh, I've got a great doctor because he shook my hand once, or I got a great doctor because he promised he would look into getting me better.

817
01:01:49,754 --> 01:01:53,156
[SPEAKER_03]: The only thing that matters is that your government actually delivers results.

818
01:01:53,576 --> 01:01:55,997
[SPEAKER_03]: And the only way you do that is by working hard,

819
01:01:56,557 --> 01:02:10,053
[SPEAKER_03]: to go out and knock on thousands of doors, as I have done, and listen to people of what they need, whether it is universal child care, because it's a second mortgage, whether it is housing that they can afford, roads that don't have massive potholes in it that blows out their tires or twist their ankles.

820
01:02:10,093 --> 01:02:12,856
[SPEAKER_03]: And both of those were just yesterday on the doors, people telling me this.

821
01:02:13,357 --> 01:02:15,719
[SPEAKER_03]: But the only way you do that is if you can go to Beacon Hill

822
01:02:16,380 --> 01:02:19,481
[SPEAKER_03]: get the votes for it, get the money for it, and then get it done.

823
01:02:19,701 --> 01:02:20,601
[SPEAKER_03]: And so that's what I want to do.

824
01:02:20,641 --> 01:02:29,323
[SPEAKER_03]: If I go, if I'm lucky enough to get your vote, there's three questions that I'm going to be asking myself every day, which is what is the problem, what is the solution, and how do we get it done?

825
01:02:29,623 --> 01:02:37,945
[SPEAKER_03]: And if you do that every single day when people are paying attention and not paying attention, that's how you get at the end of the year a little bit better than it is now and a little bit better after that.

826
01:02:38,265 --> 01:02:44,467
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's how the people who are living here get the government that they deserve and get the representative that they deserve too.

827
01:02:44,767 --> 01:02:45,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

828
01:02:45,407 --> 01:02:46,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

829
01:02:46,388 --> 01:02:54,692
[SPEAKER_01]: My fellow voters, these are the three candidates for the 34th Middlesex District, Will Embar, Chris Oates, Paul Russo.

830
01:02:55,073 --> 01:02:56,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Candidates, best wishes.

831
01:02:56,874 --> 01:02:57,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

832
01:02:57,774 --> 01:02:58,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, everybody.

